// endpoint_watch_page.proto — WatchPage / SubscribePage / UnsubscribePage: the
// ONE standing stream a browser page holds, and the two verbs that populate it.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS: A BROWSER CANNOT HOLD MANY STANDING STREAMS AT ONCE. The
// daemon serves h2c, but no browser negotiates cleartext HTTP/2, so a webview
// talks HTTP/1.1 — which caps a page at about SIX connections per host, and a
// server-streaming Connect call pins one for its whole life. The webapp held
// six (`WatchWorkspaceRoster`, `WatchWebWorkspace`, `WatchDaemon`,
// `WatchTopbar`, `WatchFooter`, `WatchDaemonHolds`) before its feed tail was
// even opened, and every stream after those SILENTLY QUEUED FOREVER: no
// request on the wire, no error, no frame. The root feed's tail was the
// seventh, so no row a workspace produced after the page loaded was ever
// drawn. Every expanded subagent bubble opens another feed tail, so the count
// grew with the conversation and no fixed budget could contain it.
//
// THE COUNT IS THE FIX, NOT THE ORDER. A page multiplexes EVERY standing
// subscription onto this one stream, so the number of connections a page holds
// is ONE no matter how many views it watches. That is a structural property of
// the protocol rather than a budget anyone has to respect: there is no second
// stream to open.
//
// THE ATTACHMENT IS A LATCH, NOT A RACE. `SubscribePage` names a page that must
// already be attached, so its first frame — `PageAttached` — is sent the moment
// the daemon has registered the page and BEFORE any subscription can exist. A
// client subscribes only after reading it; a `SubscribePage` for a page the
// daemon has not attached is REFUSED, loudly, rather than buffered against a
// stream that may never arrive.
